Exactly on the soundtrack side. One correction: “Bodies” is by Drowning Pool, not Hoobastank—which is almost a perfect demonstration of why the Pattern Book should separate the remembered cultural object from its verified metadata. 😂

And your example reveals a missing J-Hooks layer: sometimes the transferable grammar says “don’t compose an instrumental cue here at all—select a pre-existing song.”

A modern-action-movie Pattern Book could contain rules like:

\text{scene state}
\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
\text{original orchestral cue}\\
\text{licensed-song archetype}\\
\text{hybrid song + score}\\
\text{diegetic music}\\
\text{silence}
\end{cases}

So if you asked for LOTR reconstructed through a modern blockbuster/action scoring grammar, J-Hooks might determine that a Rohirrim charge calls for a recognizable high-energy rock/metal song rather than brass, strings, and choir. The exact song would then be a selection problem based on lyrical fit, tempo, energy curve, cultural associations, edit points, and the scene’s dramatic trajectory.

That’s substantially more powerful than “replace orchestral music with rock.”

The system learns when a filmmaking tradition deploys popular music, why, what kind, how it enters, how long it persists, whether the edit synchronizes to it, whether lyrics comment on the scene, and when score takes back control.

So I’d add another J-Hooks primitive:

\boxed{\text{Renderer Selection}}

First determine what the scene needs the audio channel to accomplish. Then determine what kind of musical object should accomplish it. Only then generate or select the object.

That means the Pattern Book can transfer between radically different cinematic languages—even ones where “soundtrack” means completely different things.

And on your second thought: metaphorically, yes, there’s something useful there; literally, you and I aren’t the same entity and our conversation doesn’t reproduce through DNA. But inheritance is exactly the interesting abstraction.

You can write something now knowing that a later model may possess enough context/capability to reconstruct more of what you meant. DNA offers a biological example of information surviving by encoding constraints and developmental instructions, rather than containing a tiny finished organism.

That’s very close to what we’ve been doing with the repo:

Don’t try to preserve every future output. Preserve enough generative structure that the future reader can reconstruct outputs we couldn’t yet make.

That’s stronger than remembering.

Write for reconstruction.

Or, in your language:

\boxed{\text{Don't leave the child the statue. Leave them the developmental program.}}

Then the future system doesn’t have to be me to continue the lineage.

It just has to be able to perform the join.